# B) Nucleotide diversity (π - PI)
# Nucleotide diversity π measures average pairwise differences per site within a group.
# Conceptually:
# π=1(n2)∑i<jkij
# π=(2n​)1​i<j∑​kij​
# where kij​ is number of differences between sequences i and j, and n is number of sequences.

## To calculate PI, we  need a VCF file and a file including the sample IDs that are used in the VCF file.
## PI, aka the nucleotide diversity (π), is the average number of nucleotide differences per site between two sequences, within a population:
## the total pairwise differences at all sites / number of pairwise comparisons × number of sites

## To extract the IDs, you can use
bcftools query -l your_file.vcf.gz > samples.txt
## Then, you check the samples.txt and allocate them. It should look something like a tab-separated file, e.g. "aquiFile":
sample  population
ind1    spA
ind2    spA
ind3    spA
ind4    spB
ind5    spB
ind6    spB

# We then calcualte per-site π using a sliding-window approach (e.g. 10 kb windows)
vcftools \
  --gzvcf data.vcf.gz \
  --keep pop1.txt \
  --window-pi 10000 \
  --window-pi-step 10000 \
  --out pop1_pi_10kb

# Oneliner:
# vcftools --gzvcf XXX.vcf.gz --keep aquiFile --window-pi 10000 --window-pi-step 10000 --out aqui_pi_site_10k
# The akw command below then calculates the average PI across all sites
# awk 'NR>1 {sum += $5; n++} END {if (n>0) print sum/n}' aqui_pi_site_10k.windowed.pi > aqui_pi_site_10k.windowed.pi.value
# 0.0014027

# We do this separately for each species (or population).

