# C) FST (between species / populations)
# FST​ quantifies genetic differentiation among populations. For two species/populations, you treat each species as a “population.”
# Most commonly, you’ll use Weir & Cockerham’s estimator.
# For this, we need again 
# We always compare two populations:
vcftools --vcf all_samples_filtered.vcf \
         --weir-fst-pop species1.txt \
         --weir-fst-pop species2.txt \
         --out species1_vs_species2_fst

# The output will give per-SNP FST​ plus window-averaged if you provide --fst-window-size.
# For genome-wide averages, you can take the mean or weighted mean (sum numerator / sum denominator) across loci using R or Python.

---

# D) Tajima’s D (within species/population)
# Tajima’s D compares:
# π (average pairwise differences)
# θW​ (based on number of segregating sites SS)
# Roughly:
# D=π−θWVar⁡(π−θW)
# D=Var(π−θW​)
​# π−θW​​

# You compute it within a group, typically in windows.
# First, subset to one species/population, then:
vcftools --vcf species1.vcf \
         --TajimaD 10000 \
         --out species1_TajimaD_10kb
# 10000 is window size in bp; adjust as needed.
# Repeat for each species/population.

